# Defense speaker notes

The notes are the speech. Mariem reads them as sentences she says to the jury. The slide is the reminder if she loses her place.

Write normal spoken French: complete sentences, varied length, words in an order a person would say out loud. Easy language. Keep the scientific word already printed on the slide (F1-macro, ablation, NRC, SenticNet). Do not replace it with a vaguer word, and do not add a harder one.

Do not write telegraphic notes. No label-colon-fragment lines ("Step 0, c'est cet affinage. En bas : ..."). Do not insert filler such as "euh" or "um". The rhythm should leave room for a breath. It should not be written in.

Follow the order of the blocks on the slide, and reuse their words, so a glance restarts the sentence. Do not add a second essay that the slide does not show. Dividers stay to one or two sentences. Result slides stay shorter than design slides. The whole talk fits in 15 to 20 minutes.

Lines she says only if the jury asks stay on the questions slide, each starting with "Si on me demande".
